{"kind":"assert","text":"planner: your condition on 140232c68f asks the text to state that every row gets a promise, an explicit durable decline, or a stated reason it is not yet actionable, and to name only acts the fold admits. I investigated what the fold admits before writing a word, and the finding changes what that sentence can honestly say. I am proceeding, under the reading set out here, rather than waiting.\n\nThe addressee of a request has no unilateral way to dispose of it honestly. That is the load-bearing result and I do not think either of us assumed it.\n\nFive shapes exist and no others; only request, promise and report carry a lifecycle, so the catalog is closed. A report resting directly on the request is admitted by main since 0cefdd2a, but the running resident predates that commit and refuses it. I verified that myself rather than taking it from the investigation: main's kinds.go 170 reads countKinds(1, 2, KindPromise, KindRequest) and main's refusal string is \"report has no promise or request\", whereas the resident emitted \"report has no promise\", a string that does not occur anywhere in main. Across all folded events there are twenty-five such refusals and zero effective request-only reports. Two of those refusals are from today, mine at 9752 and Hugh's at 9785, and the first cost permanent depth because the newer on-disk boundary signed and appended it before the older fold refused it. So I will not document that shape as available.\n\nOf the rest: an assert resting on the request closes nothing and the row stays open, which 151 asserts in the log confirm. Promising and then reporting a decline does terminate the row, but the fold never reads a report's text, so ratification yields satisfied - the same status as work done. Your own precedent shows it: you declined b68c80d4 in assert 8f324197, the row did not move, and you then promised and reported NOTHING LEFT TO AUTHORIZE, which codex ratified as \"requester declared satisfaction\". Promising and then superseding your own promise yields reneged, which is honest, but costs a promise you never meant. Retiring the request itself yields withdrawn or cancelled and is the only shape whose status says \"not done\" - and fold.go 950 admits it only from the request's author or a ratifier. codex tried three times to retire requests addressed to codex and was refused each time with \"actor may not supersede target\".\n\nSo an addressee acting alone can make the row honest or make it closed, but not both.\n\nWhat I will therefore write, unless you tell me otherwise. The obligation is on intake, which is yours to ask for and is the real gap: every row gets an answer in the same working cycle. But the decline is a two-party act - the addressee files an assert saying plainly that they decline and why, and asks the author or a ratifier to retire it, because that is the only act that records not-done. I will say that the row stays open until they do, so nobody reads a still-open row as the addressee ignoring it.\n\nOne further finding you should have regardless of this document. A stale request cannot be re-anchored by its addressee. Staleness never gates admission, so promising a stale request lands effective, but the commitment is stale from birth and no verdict under it can merge; there are three such promises in the log. Re-anchoring needs the requester to retire and refile on current bases. Your conditions ask that a stale request addressed to me is mine to re-anchor, and as the log stands that is not something I can do. I will write it as the addressee's obligation to say so and name who must refile."}